gned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "273c61df-da34-56fd-9a6e-fc64509e7319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364592 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364593 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4aa403fe-1986-5cce-830e-fe0040a19a01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364594 #edb/ref 1080 "R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364594 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364594 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "465339e6-0e6b-5ed2-9ec3-ea7c32d80d12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364595 #edb/ref 1080 "R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81627ba8-8d23-5e6c-b464-c7d40500c8c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364596 #edb/ref 1080 "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7b92ccf-3c22-5717-a199-7e9b189d529a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364597 #edb/ref 1080 "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8ba1bfd-3cf7-5350-80d8-d51f7211e2b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364598 #edb/ref 1080 "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364598 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364598 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24240a52-788b-5bf1-abe6-394b3adf3620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75e8d7ef-26a5-51d9-ba2c-c43a53de12e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364613 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364617 #